.TH WIFID 4
.SH NAME
wifid \- the Wi-Fi server (port "wifi")
.SH SYNOPSIS
Port
.BR wifi .
.B /bin/wifid
is started at boot by
.BR init (8),
with device rights, so the port is there to find; on a machine with no
Wi-Fi chip it ends with status 0 and is not started again.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B wifid
is a second network interface for
.BR netd (4),
and keeps the chosen network.
One chip driver per architecture does the rest:
on arm64 the Raspberry Pis' CYW43455 on SDIO (its firmware joins and runs
the handshake); on x86-64 an Intel AX210 on PCIe (SIEOS joins and runs the
WPA2 handshake itself).
.PP
To netd it looks like a network card: it answers
.B NIC_INFO
and
.B NIC_SEND
as
.BR vnet (4)
does, and sends
.B NET_FRAMES
and
.B NET_LINK
to "net".
The chosen network is joined again at every start.
.SH MESSAGES
.TP
.B WIFI_STATUS
rbuf <\- wifi_status_t: the state (off, idle, scanning, joining, up,
failed), signal in dBm, channel, security, the network's name, why the last
attempt failed, the addresses, the chip and its firmware. \-> 0.
.TP
.B WIFI_SCAN
look for networks (a few seconds); rbuf <\- wifi_net_t[] (name, BSSID,
channel, signal, security) \-> how many.
.TP
.B WIFI_JOIN
(root) sbuf = "ssid\e0passphrase" (empty: an open network): saved in
.B /etc/wifi.conf
and joined; WIFI_STATUS follows it. \-> 0.
.TP
.B WIFI_FORGET
(root) leave the network and forget it. \-> 0.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /etc/wifi.conf
the chosen network and its passphrase (root only)
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR wifi (1),
.BR wifi.conf (5),
.BR netd (4)
